rac{{sec}^{3}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{1+{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta }=$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the secant-tangent identity:\n\n$$\n1+{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta ={sec}^{2}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe rewrite the given expression using the secant-tangent identity as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{{sec}^{3}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{1+{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta }=\\frac{{sec}^{3}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta }\n$$\n\nWe now simplify the expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{sec}^{3}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta } & =\\frac{{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\cancel{{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta }} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we recall the following identities: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta },\\,\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta },\n$$\n\n \n\nUsing the above identities, we can further simplify our expression as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{{sec}^{3}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{1+{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta }=\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta .\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-120639/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "91100afb-9a36-57c0-9f30-4692cfef0b93"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-120639"] :db/ensure [:knowledg